1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,323
A kite tugs on its string.

2
00:00:02,323 --> 00:00:04,442
A box scrapes across the floor.

3
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:05,903
A kicked ball flies.

4
00:00:05,903 --> 00:00:08,482
Each has several forces on it at once.

5
00:00:08,882 --> 00:00:12,298
To untangle them, physicists
use one simple drawing.

6
00:00:12,748 --> 00:00:16,236
Aarav drags a box of books
down the corridor, with a rope.

7
00:00:16,586 --> 00:00:17,964
The box speeds up.

8
00:00:17,964 --> 00:00:19,858
How many forces act on it?

9
00:00:19,858 --> 00:00:22,050
Most people say one, his pull.

10
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:23,478
The answer is four.

11
00:00:23,978 --> 00:00:26,939
Let's find all four,
with a free-body diagram.

12
00:00:27,589 --> 00:00:32,048
A free-body diagram shows one body,
cut free from everything around it.

13
00:00:32,398 --> 00:00:36,397
We draw the body as a box, and
every force on it as an arrow.

14
00:00:36,797 --> 00:00:38,656
Only forces on the body.

15
00:00:38,656 --> 00:00:41,057
Never forces it exerts on others.

16
00:00:41,457 --> 00:00:45,684
Then we add the forces along each
direction, and use the second law.

17
00:00:46,334 --> 00:00:47,733
To the motion notebook.

18
00:00:47,733 --> 00:00:51,391
A two kilogram block on a rough
floor, with a rope tied to it.

19
00:00:51,741 --> 00:00:53,543
First, isolate the block.

20
00:00:53,543 --> 00:00:55,042
Everything else fades.

21
00:00:55,442 --> 00:00:57,953
Force one: its weight, straight down.

22
00:00:57,953 --> 00:01:01,834
m g, two times nine point eight,
nineteen point six newtons.

23
00:01:02,234 --> 00:01:04,449
Force two: the floor pushes up.

24
00:01:04,449 --> 00:01:08,988
The normal force, also nineteen point
six, since the block does not move

25
00:01:08,988 --> 00:01:09,949
up or down.

26
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:12,862
Force three: the rope pulls it forward.

27
00:01:12,862 --> 00:01:14,561
The tension, ten newtons.

28
00:01:14,962 --> 00:01:18,110
Force four: friction from
the floor, pulling back.

29
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:19,900
Six newtons while it slides.

30
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,060
Up and down, they cancel.

31
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:25,247
Along the floor, ten minus
six leaves four newtons.

32
00:01:25,247 --> 00:01:28,444
a is four over two, two
metres per second squared.

33
00:01:29,044 --> 00:01:30,057
Pull harder.

34
00:01:30,057 --> 00:01:32,032
Fourteen newtons gives four.

35
00:01:32,032 --> 00:01:33,474
Eighteen gives six.

36
00:01:33,874 --> 00:01:36,723
On the graph, the dots
sit on a straight line.

37
00:01:36,723 --> 00:01:38,669
Its slope is one over the mass.

38
00:01:39,269 --> 00:01:40,370
Slow-mo replay.

39
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:42,584
Freeze, and check every arrow.

40
00:01:42,934 --> 00:01:43,901
Four forces.

41
00:01:43,901 --> 00:01:46,993
The block's own push on
the floor is not here.

42
00:01:46,993 --> 00:01:48,589
It acts on the floor.

43
00:01:49,189 --> 00:01:50,104
Your turn.

44
00:01:50,104 --> 00:01:51,345
The rope snaps.

45
00:01:51,345 --> 00:01:54,659
Which forces act on the
block as it slides on?

46
00:01:54,659 --> 00:01:56,005
Pause and decide.

47
00:01:59,005 --> 00:02:01,345
Weight, normal force and friction!

48
00:02:01,345 --> 00:02:02,778
No force of motion.

49
00:02:02,778 --> 00:02:07,129
Friction alone acts along the
floor, so the block slows and stops.

50
00:02:07,779 --> 00:02:09,967
Now the method, one step at a time.

51
00:02:10,317 --> 00:02:11,217
Step one.

52
00:02:11,106 --> 00:02:13,434
Pick one body, and draw it alone.

53
00:02:14,184 --> 00:02:15,084
Step two.

54
00:02:14,937 --> 00:02:16,497
Draw every force on it.

55
00:02:16,497 --> 00:02:19,608
Weight first, then one for
each thing touching it.

56
00:02:20,358 --> 00:02:21,341
Step three.

57
00:02:21,341 --> 00:02:24,800
Choose axes: x along
the motion, y across it.

58
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:26,598
Step four.

59
00:02:26,598 --> 00:02:29,502
Along y, there is no acceleration.

60
00:02:29,502 --> 00:02:31,695
So N minus m g is zero.

61
00:02:31,695 --> 00:02:33,069
N equals m g.

62
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:34,792
Step five.

63
00:02:34,792 --> 00:02:38,206
Along x, the sum of the forces equals m a.

64
00:02:38,206 --> 00:02:40,300
So T minus f equals m a.

65
00:02:41,050 --> 00:02:42,116
Remember this.

66
00:02:42,116 --> 00:02:46,433
Draw only the forces on the body,
then add them along each axis.

67
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:49,254
Here is a test for every arrow.

68
00:02:49,254 --> 00:02:50,285
Link by link.

69
00:02:50,635 --> 00:02:52,187
Each force needs a giver.

70
00:02:52,187 --> 00:02:53,087
The weight?

71
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:54,040
The Earth pulls.

72
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:56,256
The normal force and friction?

73
00:02:56,256 --> 00:02:58,072
The floor, where it touches.

74
00:02:58,472 --> 00:02:59,372
The tension?

75
00:02:59,211 --> 00:03:00,111
The rope.

76
00:03:00,236 --> 00:03:01,136
No giver?

77
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:02,219
Then no force.

78
00:03:02,219 --> 00:03:05,057
That is why there is no force of motion.

79
00:03:05,657 --> 00:03:06,659
Side by side.

80
00:03:06,659 --> 00:03:10,958
Left: forces on the block, from
the Earth, the floor and the rope.

81
00:03:10,958 --> 00:03:12,536
They go in its diagram.

82
00:03:12,936 --> 00:03:14,978
Right: the forces by the block.

83
00:03:14,978 --> 00:03:18,600
It presses the floor, pulls
the rope, and pulls the Earth.

84
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:20,451
They belong to other diagrams.

85
00:03:21,051 --> 00:03:22,136
Now you try.

86
00:03:22,136 --> 00:03:24,737
A kicked ball flies through the air.

87
00:03:24,737 --> 00:03:25,706
Ignore air.

88
00:03:25,706 --> 00:03:28,034
Which forces are on its diagram?

89
00:03:28,034 --> 00:03:29,382
Pause and decide.

90
00:03:32,582 --> 00:03:33,491
Weight only!

91
00:03:33,491 --> 00:03:35,808
The foot no longer touches the ball.

92
00:03:35,808 --> 00:03:37,984
Only the Earth still pulls on it.

93
00:03:38,634 --> 00:03:40,370
Now, one worked example.

94
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:45,305
Aarav lifts a five kilogram box with
a rope, speeding it up at two metres

95
00:03:45,305 --> 00:03:46,652
per second squared.

96
00:03:46,652 --> 00:03:47,900
Find the tension.

97
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:50,857
Two forces: tension up, weight down.

98
00:03:50,857 --> 00:03:54,725
The weight is five times nine
point eight, forty nine newtons.

99
00:03:55,475 --> 00:03:56,773
Up is positive.

100
00:03:56,773 --> 00:03:59,833
T minus forty nine equals five times two.

101
00:04:00,583 --> 00:04:02,428
So T is fifty nine newtons.

102
00:04:02,428 --> 00:04:06,259
More than the weight, because
the box speeds up as it rises.

103
00:04:06,909 --> 00:04:08,374
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:08,724 --> 00:04:12,952
Many students add the block's push
on the floor to its own diagram.

105
00:04:13,352 --> 00:04:15,618
But that force acts on the floor.

106
00:04:15,618 --> 00:04:18,384
The diagram holds only forces on the body.

107
00:04:19,034 --> 00:04:20,459
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:20,459 --> 00:04:25,260
In almost every laws of motion numerical:
blocks, ropes, pulleys and lifts.

109
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:27,086
Exam level now.

110
00:04:27,086 --> 00:04:31,759
A four kilogram block on a smooth
floor is pulled by a rope with twenty

111
00:04:31,759 --> 00:04:35,425
newtons, at thirty seven
degrees above the horizontal.

112
00:04:35,425 --> 00:04:38,748
Sine thirty seven is
point six, and g is ten.

113
00:04:38,748 --> 00:04:41,676
Find the normal force
and the acceleration.

114
00:04:41,676 --> 00:04:42,953
Pause and solve.

115
00:04:48,353 --> 00:04:49,618
The answer is B.

116
00:04:49,618 --> 00:04:53,283
Twenty eight newtons, and four
metres per second squared.

117
00:04:53,783 --> 00:04:58,585
Three forces: weight forty down, the
normal force up, and the rope's twenty

118
00:04:58,585 --> 00:05:00,723
newtons at thirty seven degrees.

119
00:05:01,223 --> 00:05:05,008
Along y: N plus twenty times
point six equals forty.

120
00:05:05,008 --> 00:05:06,981
N is twenty eight newtons.

121
00:05:07,481 --> 00:05:10,330
Along x: cos thirty seven is point eight.

122
00:05:10,330 --> 00:05:12,715
Twenty times point eight is sixteen.

123
00:05:12,715 --> 00:05:16,109
Over four kilograms, four
metres per second squared.

124
00:05:16,709 --> 00:05:18,170
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:19,671
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:22,871 --> 00:05:24,801
Can you answer these without help?

127
00:05:25,201 --> 00:05:26,101
One.

128
00:05:25,726 --> 00:05:29,809
How many forces act on Aarav's
box, and who gives each one?

129
00:05:30,709 --> 00:05:31,609
Two.

130
00:05:31,203 --> 00:05:35,102
Why is the block's push on the
floor left out of its diagram?

131
00:05:36,002 --> 00:05:36,902
Three.

132
00:05:36,581 --> 00:05:38,547
A box speeds up as it rises.

133
00:05:38,547 --> 00:05:41,139
Why is the tension more than its weight?

134
00:05:42,039 --> 00:05:45,107
Next time: Aarav hangs his bag on a rope.

135
00:05:45,107 --> 00:05:47,955
He pulls it tight, but it always sags.

136
00:05:47,955 --> 00:05:48,855
Why?

137
00:05:48,487 --> 00:05:50,084
Equilibrium of forces.
